WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.500
Welcome to this functional 3D anatomical tour of the femoral triangle and femoral canal in the upper anterior thigh.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:18.200
The femoral triangle is a subfascial anatomical space situated in the superomedial aspect of the anterior thigh.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:29.000
Its superior boundary is formed by the inguinal ligament, extending from the anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS) to the pubic tubercle.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:39.500
The lateral boundary is defined by the medial border of the sartorius muscle, the longest strap muscle in the human body.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:50.000
Medially, the triangle is bounded by the medial margin of the adductor longus muscle, which forms the inner apex of the triangle.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:01:03.000
The muscular floor of the femoral triangle is formed from lateral to medial by the iliopsoas tendon, the pectineus muscle, and adductor longus.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:15.500
The roof of the triangle consists of the fascia lata and the cribriform fascia, pierced by the great saphenous vein as it drains into the femoral vein.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:28.000
The contents of the femoral triangle from lateral to medial follow the classic surgical mnemonic NAVEL: Nerve, Artery, Vein, Empty space, Lymphatics.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:42.000
Note that the femoral nerve (L2, L3, L4 posterior divisions) lies lateral to the femoral sheath, outside the sheath compartments.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:56.500
The femoral sheath is a downward fascial prolongation of the transversalis fascia anteriorly and the fascia iliaca posteriorly enclosing the vessels.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:10.000
Within the sheath, the lateral compartment contains the femoral artery and femoral branch of the genitofemoral nerve.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:22.000
The intermediate compartment houses the femoral vein, receiving the profunda femoris vein and great saphenous tributary.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:37.000
The medial compartment is the femoral canal, a conical potential space approximately 1.3 centimeters in length.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:51.000
The upper opening of the femoral canal is the femoral ring, bounded by the inguinal ligament anteriorly and the pectineal (Cooper's) ligament posteriorly.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:03:04.000
The lacunar ligament forms the medial crescent boundary, while the femoral vein septum bounds the ring laterally.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:17.000
The femoral canal contains loose adipose connective tissue and the deep inguinal lymph node of Cloquet (Rosenmüller).

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:30.000
Clinical significance: Increased intra-abdominal pressure can force abdominal viscera through the narrow femoral ring into the canal, causing a femoral hernia.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:40.800
Femoral hernias have a high propensity for strangulation due to the rigid, sharp margin of the lacunar ligament, requiring prompt surgical intervention.
